21:42:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily. A centre of mass is well-defined only when certain conditions hold. In ordinary physics, you calculate it by averaging the positions of all the mass: \[ \mathbf r_{\rm CM}=\frac{\sum m_i\mathbf r_i}{\sum m_i}. \] That assumes there is a meaningful shared coordinate system in which all the objects have positions, and that the total mass and the average are finite and well-defined. For the whole universe, those assumptions may fail: - **If the universe is infinite and roughly uniform**, it contains infinitely much matter in every direction. There is no finite “average position” of all its mass, so no global centre of mass. - **If space is finite but curved and has no boundary**, such as the three-dimensional analogue of a sphere’s surface, there need not be a central point *in the space itself*. A sphere’s surface has mass and is finite, but no point on the surface is its centre of mass. You can speak of a centre only by imagining the surface embedded in a higher-dimensional outside space. - **In general relativity**, mass-energy and spacetime geometry are linked, and a universal, coordinate-independent centre of mass is not generally defined for an expanding entire universe the way it is for a baseball or solar system. There *are* centres of mass for local, bounded systems: Earth, the Solar System, the Milky Way, galaxy clusters, and so on. Those are physical objects with identifiable boundaries and finite mass. You can also define a centre of mass for the **observable universe**, after choosing a coordinate system and specifying which matter is included. But that would be a property of our observable patch, not proof of a unique centre of the whole universe or the location of the Big Bang. Another distant observer’s observable patch would be centered differently. So “everything physical has a centre of mass” is a good approximation for everyday finite objects in ordinary space—not a rule that automatically applies to spacetime as a whole.
